L000058	Division of Theoretical Physics, Technische Universit\"at Berlin, Hardenbergstrasse 36,
L000059	10623, Berlin, Germany}
L000060	
L000061	
L000062	
L000063	\date{\today}
L000064	
L000065	\begin{abstract}
L000066	The time dynamics of flagellar and ciliary beating is often neglected in theories of microswimmers, with the most common models prescribing a time-constant actuation of the surrounding fluid. By explicitly introducing a metachronal wave, coarse-grained to a sinusoidal surface slip velocity, we show that a
L000067	spatial resonance between the metachronal wave and the corrugation of a confining cylindrical channel enables a ciliate to swim even when it cannot move forward in a bulk fluid.
L000068	Using lubrication theory, we reduce the problem to the Adler equation that reveals an oscillatory and steady swimming regime. Interestingly, a ciliate can even reverse its swimming direction in a corrugated channel compared to the bulk fluid.
L000069	\end{abstract}
L000070	
L000071	\maketitle
L000072	
L000073	
L000074	
L000075	
L000076	\begin{figure}[t]
L000077		\centering
L000078	   \includegraphics[width=1\columnwidth]{images/model4.png}
L000079	   \caption{(a)
L000080	   Schematic of a long ciliate (green cylinder) that swims in a cylindrical channel with corrugated walls, which are depicted in black while the surrounding fluid is drawn in blue. The cilia are coarse-grained to an effective slip velocity $v_c$, represented by purple
L000081	   arrows on the surface of the ciliate, while the ciliate moves with velocity $v_p$. (b) Schematics of a reciprocal (above) and non-reciprocal (below) ciliary beat pattern. Pink arrows indicate the direction of motion of the single cilia. The metachronal wave travels from left to right with speed $v_m >0$. For a sufficiently sparse ciliary carpet, the net fluid flow of the reciprocal beat pattern is negligible.
L000082	   }
L000083	        	\label{fig:model}
L000084	\end{figure}
L000085	
L000086	It is widely known that bodies undergoing a reciprocal motion cannot propel in bulk fluids at low Reynolds numbers \cite{Purcell1977}. Thus, microorganisms need to rely on structures such as flagella \cite{Mukherjee2014} or cilia \cite{Lin2014} which beat in a non-reciprocal fashion \cite{Purcell1977,Lauga2011}.
L000087	Due to the challenge of fully capturing the dynamics of each flagellum or cilium, theoretical descriptions of microswimmers typically rely on a coarse-graining. Examples include the specification
L000088	of an effective surface slip velocity in the well-known squirmer model \cite{Lighthill1952,Blake1971,Ishikawa2024, Chisholm2016, Zantop2020,Ganguly2025}
L000089	or a truncated multipolar expansion for the swimmer-generated flow fields
L000090	\cite{Lauga2012,Lauga2016,Ishikawa2024, Yeomans2014,Drescher2010}. Such models have been successful in shedding light on the behavior of microswimmers in complex environments, including swimmer
L000091	suspensions \cite{Pagonabarraga2013, Alarcon2013, Shum2025, Ishikawa2007, Ishikawa2008,Yoshinaga2017},
L000092	also under confinement by flat \cite{Ahana_2019,WuZhang2024, HernandezOrtiz_2009, Llopis2010,Ishimoto2013,Ruhle2018, Shen2018,Zantop2020,Ruhle2020,Lintuvuori2016} or curved \cite{Kurzthaler2021,Ishimoto2023,Kuron2019,Chaithanya2021, Ishikawa2018, Barri2022,Zheng2023} walls,
L000093	as well as
L000094	
L000095	porous \cite{Chamolly2017,Kamarapu2022} and deformable \cite{Shaik2017} materials.
L000096	
L000097	However, nearly all of the cited theoretical work focuses on the time-averaged actuation of the fluid by the flagellar or ciliary beat, \emph{i.e.}, prescribes slip velocity profiles and multipolar expansion coefficients that are constant in time.
L000098	While such descriptions may capture the dynamics of microswimming on long timescales, they fail to describe the dynamics on the timescale of the beating period \cite{Klindt2015,Pedley2016,Guasto2010,Redaelli2023}. In this Letter, we show that accounting for the time dynamics of ciliary beating is crucial to understanding the motion of ciliates in spatially inhomogeneous confinement. In particular, their ability to move forward
L000099	is heavily effected by a spatio-temporal resonance between the fluid forcing
L000100	coming from the ciliary beating pattern and the modulated environment.
L000101	
L000102	
L000103	
L000104	In our studies, we use both lattice-Boltzmann simulations and a theoretical analysis based on the lubrication approximation. The latter is a versatile tool and has been used extensively in different contexts such as biological flows \cite{Lubrication_Stegemerten2022,Ureter_Zheng2021,Ureter_Lykoudis1970,Kozlov2015}, fluid-mediated interactions \cite{Dowson1962, Patel2022,Davis1986,Rallabandi2024},  chemically-active \cite{Antunes2022, Antunes2023, Antunes2024,Lubrication_Richter2025,Lubrication_Voss2024} or charged \cite{Bai2006,Malgaretti2014} flows, as well as droplets and films \cite{Lubrication_Thiele2003, Lubrication_Witelski2000,Lubrication_Yang2025,Lubrication_Charitatos2020}.
L000105	
L000106	In the following, we demonstrate how a sinusoidal metachronal wave coarse-grained to a surface slip velocity that spatially resonates with the corrugation of a confining channel enables ciliates to swim, even when they cannot move forward in a bulk fluid. In particular, we reduce the swimming dynamics to the Adler equation that shows an oscillatory and a steady regime.
L000107	
L000108	Specifically, we aim to investigate the motion of a ciliate in a cylindrical channel aligned with the $x$ axis \footnote{In our work, we consider straight channels to bring out the basic mechanism of the novel phenomenology. However, it also applies
L000109	to channels with curvature radii
L000110	
L000111	
L000112	much larger than the dimensions of the ciliate.}
L000113	 and whose local radius $R(x)$ varies according to
L000114	\begin{equation}
L000115	    \label{eq:Radius}
L000116	    R(x) = R_c + R_0 + R_1 \cos(k_w x) \, .
L000117	\end{equation}
L000118	Here, $R_c$ is the radius of the ciliate, $R_0$ is the spatially averaged distance between the ciliate and the channel wall,
L000119	$R_1$ is the amplitude of the corrugation, and $k_w$ is its wavenumber [see Fig. \ref{fig:model} (a)]. The ciliate is modeled as a cylinder whose length $L_c$ is much larger than the wavelength $\lambda_w = 2\pi/k_w$ and so can be considered effectively infinite in length.
L000120	Ciliates are covered by a carpet of beating cilia, which move fluid along the ciliate surface and thereby propel the microorganism forward. Because variations in height of the ciliary layer are typically much smaller than $R_c$ \cite{Paramecium_Jana2012}, we coarse-grain
L000121	the operation of this layer into an effective slip velocity tangential to the surface of the ciliate,
L000122	\begin{equation}
L000123	    \mathbf{v}(x,r=R_c,t) = [v_p(t) + v_c(x,t)] \mathbf{e}_x,
L000124	    \label{eq_active_bc}
L000125	\end{equation}
L000126	
L000127	Here, $\mathbf{v}(x,r,t)$ is the velocity of the fluid constrained between the ciliate and the corrugated channel wall, $v_p(t)$ is the swimming velocity of the ciliate, which needs to be determined, and the slip velocity
L000128	$v_c(x,t)$ encodes the ciliary beating. Contrary to previous work, we do not take the velocity $v_c(x,t)$ to be constant in time, as we aim to capture the time-varying actuation by the ciliary layer and its effect on the surrounding fluid. Indeed, cilia beat in a periodic fashion with period $T_c$ and their motions
L000129	often split into a power and recovery stroke \cite{Gray1922, Lin2014} [see Fig.\ \ref{fig:model}(b)] that are non-reciprocal, enabling net swimming and motion of fluid.
L000130	
L000131	Typically, neighboring cilia beat with a phase lag and thereby form a metachronal wave \cite{Brumley2012,Guirao2007,Poon2023,Elgeti2013}
L000132	with a wavelength $\lambda_c$ much smaller than the size $L_c$ of the ciliate \cite{Paramecium_Jana2012}. To capture these features, we propose for the slip velocity at the surface of the ciliate,
L000133	\begin{equation}
L000134	    \label{eq:slip_velocity}
L000135	    v_c(x,t) = v_0 + v_1 \cos\{k_c [x-x_p(t)] - \omega t\},
L000136	\end{equation}
L000137	where $v_0$ is the time-averaged velocity imposed by the ciliary layer, and $v_1$ is the amplitude of the metachronal wave. Here, $v_0 \neq 0$ indicates that the ciliary carpet induces a net fluid transport across the surface of the ciliate. Such a transport may result from non-reciprocal power and recovery strokes or even from reciprocally beating cilia that
L000138	are packed densely enough so that hydrodynamic interactions lead to net fluid flow \cite{Khaderi2012}.
L000139	Furthermore, $k_c = 2\pi/\lambda_c$ is the wavenumber of the metachronal wave and $\omega = 2\pi/T_c$ is the ciliary frequency. As a result, the metachronal wave travels with a speed $v_m=\omega/k_c$ in the body-fixed frame of the ciliate. The
L000140	origin of this frame of reference moves with velocity $v_p(t)$ along the $x$ direction and is located at position $ x_p(t) = \int_0^t v_p(t^{\prime}) dt^{\prime}$.
L000141	
L000142	Now, the main task is to determine the swimming velocity of the ciliate, $v_p(t)$. At low Reynolds numbers, the fluid obeys Stokes' equations and the incompressibility condition,
L000143	 $\nabla P(\mathbf{r},t) = \mu \Delta \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{r},t)$ and $ \nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{r},t) = 0 $, where $\mu$ is the dynamic viscosity and $P(\mathbf{r},t)$ is the pressure. No-slip boundary conditions apply at the surface of the channel, $  \mathbf{v}(x,r=R(x),t) = 0 $. The ciliate swims in an otherwise quiescent fluid such that no external pressure gradient is applied, and moves force-free meaning without an applied external force, $ \oint_S \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{r},t) \cdot \mathbf{n}(\mathbf{r},t)  dS = \mathbf{0}
L000144	$,
L000145	where $S$ is the surface of the ciliate, $\boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{r},t)$ is the Newtonian stress tensor, $    \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{r},t) = -P(\mathbf{r},t) \mathbf{I} + \mu [\nabla \otimes \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{r},t) + (\nabla \otimes \mathbf{v})^T(\mathbf{r},t)]   $,
L000146	and $\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{r},t)$ is the outer normal.
L000147	
L000148	\begin{figure}
L000149		\centering
L000150	   \includegraphics[width= 1 \columnwidth]{images/purely_lubrication.pdf}
L000151	   \caption{Resonance between confinement and metachronal wave. (a) Position $x_p$ of the ciliate plotted \emph{vs.} time for different values of $v_0/v_m$ and $v_1/v_m$ with $\mathcal{G}_n=2$. Colors indicate value of $v_0/v_m$ and line types indicate $v_1/v_m$. (b) Snapshot of the fluid flow for $v_0=0$, $R_0/R_c = 3/5$, $R_1/R_0=1/3$, $R_0/\lambda_c=3/20$, $v_1/v_m=3.6$, taken at $t/T_c = 3.78$.
L000152	    The fluid streamlines are shown in black and the color indicates the flow-velocity component $v_x$. In this moment, the ciliate
L000153	    moves to the left. (c) Time-averaged ciliate velocity $\langle v_p \rangle$ and (d) period of oscillatory motion $T$ plotted
L000154	     \emph{vs.}
L000155	     the amplitude of ciliary slip velocity, $v_1/v_m$, times the amplitude of channel corrugation, $R_1/R_0$, for several values of $v_0/v_m$. In all panels $\lambda_c = \lambda_w$.
L000156	}
L000157	    	\label{fig:purely_lubrication}
L000158	\end{figure}
L000159	
L000160	\emph{Lubrication theory and linearization}.
L000161	We solve the above equations by employing the lubrication approximation \cite{Schlichting1979}, whereby the velocity field is taken
L000162	to vary much more rapidly in the radial direction than in the $x$ direction. Such an assumption is true if the characteristic lengths
L000163	in the radial direction ($R_0$, $R_1$) are much smaller than the characteristic lengths along the $x$ axis ($\lambda_w, \lambda_c$).
L000164	Ultimately, we arrive at the following equation for the time evolution of $x_p(t)$,
L000165	\begin{equation}
L000166	  \dot{x}_p(t) = -\langle \mathcal{G}(x) v_c(x,t) \rangle \, ,
L000167	 \label{eq_withMobility}
L000168	 \end{equation}
L000169	where the angle brackets indicate an average over $x$, and the local mobility coefficient $\mathcal{G}(x)$ only depends on the shape
L000170	of the channel wall and the radius of the ciliate (see Supplemental Material in Sect. I ).
L000171	In particular, $\langle \mathcal{G}(x) \rangle =1$. Since $v_c(x,t)$ is a sinusoid [Eq. \eqref{eq:slip_velocity}], one immediately obtains
L000172	\begin{equation}
L000173	     \dot{x}_p(t) =
L000174	  \begin{cases}
L000175	\displaystyle
L000176	 -v_0 - v_1 \frac{ \mathcal{G}_n}{2}\cos\left[  k_c x_p(t) + \omega t \right]  &\text{if }  \lambda_c=\lambda_w/n \\
L000177	- v_0 & \text{if } \lambda_c \neq \lambda_w/n \, , \label{eq_lubTheory}
L000178	  \end{cases}
L000179	\end{equation}
L000180	where $n$ is a natural number and $\mathcal{G}_n$ are the respective Fourier coefficients of $\mathcal{G}(x)$.
L000181	In the limits of small curvature of the ciliate body ($R_0/R_c \ll 1$) and shallow
L000182	corrugation ($R_1/R_0 \ll 1$), a linearization with respect to these parameters shows that only the
L000183	Fourier coefficient for $n=1$ is non-zero, becoming $\mathcal{G}_1 = 2R_1/R_0$. Clearly, in the case where the spatiotemporal dynamics of the ciliary
L000184	carpet is discarded ($v_1=0$), the channel is flat ($R_1 =0$), or the wavelengths of the metachronal wave and the corrugation do
L000185	not match ($\lambda_c \neq \lambda_w/n$), the ciliate swims with a velocity $v_p = - v_0$, as it would when placed in bulk fluid.
L000186	However, if none of the three previous conditions are met, a resonance between the local hydrodynamic resistance (resulting from
L000187	the ccorrugated wall profile) and the fluid forcing (resulting from the metachronal wave) generates the extra term in Eq.\ \eqref{eq_lubTheory}.
L000188	The effect on the trajectory of the ciliate can be seen by numerically solving Eq.\ \eqref{eq_lubTheory}, the results of which we show
L000189	in Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication}(a). When $v_1/v_m =0.7$ (full lines), where $v_m$ is the metachronal-wave speed, the ciliate swims with an overall drift and superimposed oscillations.
L000190	
L000191	Remarkably, such a drift occurs even when the ciliary carpet does not generate net fluid transport along its surface [$v_0=0$, magenta lines
L000192	 in Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication}(a)]. An organism with $v_0=0$ cannot propel itself in a bulk fluid, but it is able to when placed in a
L000193	 corrugated channel of appropriate wavelength. The cause is an imbalance between the net propulsion generated by the power stroke and
L000194	 the recovery stroke. While both generate the same slip velocity (in magnitude), they are performed in regions of different film thickness,
L000195	 where the generated flow experiences different effective hydrodynamic resistances [see Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication}(b)]. The result is
L000196	 directed motion even when the time-and-space-averaged slip velocity is zero. Every pair of forward and backward beating cilia can
L000197	 be thought of as two people playing tug-of-war.
L000198	Even if
L000199	 they are equally strong, the pair will still move if one person stands on dry concrete and the other on slippery mud.
L000200	Thus, motion is
L000201	 enabled by a variation in the friction profile, which occurs on a length scale comparable to the rope's length. Depending on the phase
L000202	 difference between the corrugated
L000203	 wall and the velocity profile on the surface of the ciliate, the ciliate swims either with or against the metachronal wave. What enables net
L000204	propulsion (rather than oscillation about a certain position) is the fact that the metachronal wave has a given direction. Because the
L000205	velocity of the metachronal wave in the laboratory frame is the sum of the velocities of the ciliate velocity and of the metachronal wave,
L000206	configurations of the flow leading to swimming against the wave persist longer than configurations which lead to swimming with the wave.
L000207	As such, the ciliate spends more time swimming against the metachronal wave than with it, as can be seen in Supplemental Video 1 .
L000208	
L000209	For high enough values of
L000210	$v_1$, the trajectory is no longer oscillatory, but instead becomes steady, in the direction opposite
L000211	to the metachronal wave, and exactly at its speed [see Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication} (a)].
L000212	Thus, no matter how strongly the ciliary carpet may push, the ciliate is constrained
L000213	to swim with the metachronal phase speed $v_m$. In this regime, the slip velocity profile becomes stationary in the laboratory frame
L000214	of reference, as can be seen in Supplemental Video 2 . Thus, the phase difference between the channel corrugation and the slip velocity profile is constant, and no further acceleration is possible.
L000215	
L000216	
L000217	One can indeed rationalize the two observed dynamical regimes by introducing the non-dimensional coordinate in the frame of reference
L000218	moving with the metachronal wave,  $\xi = k_c x_p(t)  + \omega t + \pi/2$. Rescaling time by $\omega^{-1}$, Eq.\ (\ref{eq_lubTheory}) for $\lambda_c = \lambda_w/n$ can be cast in the form of the well-known Adler equation \cite{Strogatz2015, Adler1946, Grawitter2024},
L000219	\begin{equation}
L000220	\dot{\xi} = a - b\sin \xi \quad \text{with} \enspace a = 1- \frac{v_0}{v_m} \enspace \text{and} \enspace
L000221	b =  \frac{v_1}{v_m}
L000222	\frac{\mathcal{G}_n}{2} \, ,
L000223	\label{eq_Adler}
L000224	\end{equation}
L000225	which indeed shows a dynamical transition for
L000226	\begin{equation}
L000227	\left | \frac{a}{b} \right | = \left |  \left (1- \frac{v_0}{v_m}\right ) \Big/  \left ( \frac{v_1}{v_m}
L000228	\frac{\mathcal{G}_n}{2}
L000229	\right) \right | = 1 \, .
L000230	\end{equation}
L000231	It is now possible to infer the time-averaged velocity of the ciliate, $\langle v_p \rangle$ \cite{Strogatz2015, Grawitter2024}. In the phase-locked regime ($|a/b| < 1$), one obtains $\langle v_p \rangle = - v_m $,
L000232	while the oscillatory
L000233	regime ($|a/b| > 1$) yields
L000234	\begin{equation}
L000235	\frac{\langle v_p \rangle}{v_m} = \left(1 - \frac{v_0}{v_m} \right)
L000236	\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{\frac{v_1}{v_m}\frac{\mathcal{G}_n}{2}}{1-\frac{v_0}{v_m}}\right)^2} -1  \, .
L000237	\label{eq.linearized}
L000238	\end{equation}
L000239	
L000240	As mentioned earlier, in the linearized limit,
L000241	$\mathcal{G}_n = 2R_1/R_0 \, \delta_{1n}$, where $\delta_{ij}$ is the Kronecker symbol.
L000242	So, in Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication} (c), we plot the time-averaged ciliate velocity \emph{versus} $v_1/v_m \times R_1/R_0$ for
L000243	different $v_0/v_m$ and $\lambda_c=\lambda_w$. For increasing
L000244	$v_1R_1$,  the ciliate velocity approaches that of the metachronal wave. Depending on
L000245	$v_0$, $\langle v_p \rangle$ either
L000246	decreases or increases towards $-v_m$. In fact, the ciliate even reverses direction with respect to the bulk fluid if $v_0 /v_m < 0$ and
L000247	$v_1/v_m \times R_1/R_0 > \sqrt{a^2-1}$.
L000248	Thus, an antiplectic ciliate, which swims in the same direction as the metachronal wave in the bulk fluid (\emph{e.g.} \textit{Paramecium} \cite{Funfak2014}), may become a symplectic swimmer, which moves opposite to the metachronal wave (\emph{e.g.} \textit{Opalina} \cite{Tamm1970}) in a sufficiently corrugated channel.
L000249	
L000250	The period of the oscillatory motion, $T$, can also be obtained analytically \cite{Strogatz2015, Grawitter2024},
L000251	\begin{equation}
L000252	T = \frac{T_c}{\sqrt{\left( \frac{v_0}{v_m} -1 \right)^2 - \left( \frac{v_1}{v_m}
L000253	\frac{\mathcal{G}_n}{2}\right)^2}} \, .
L000254	\end{equation}
L000255	It diverges at the transition from the oscillatory to the phase-locked regime, and takes values both below and above $T_c$, as shown in Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication}(d).
L000256	
L000257	\begin{figure}[t]
L000258		\centering
L000259	   \includegraphics[width= 1\columnwidth]{images/effect_R1R0_R0Rc.pdf}
L000260	   \caption{
L000261	   Confinement-induced motion. Time-averaged ciliate velocity $\langle v_p \rangle$
L000262	   as function of (a) ciliary wave amplitude $v_1/v_m$, (b) corrugation amplitude
L000263	   $R_1/R_0$, and (c) ciliate curvature $R_0/R_c$ for $\lambda_c / \lambda_w=1$, $R_0/\lambda_c=1/4$, and $v_0 = 0$. The two varied parameters of the curves are indicated in the legends by color and line style, respectively. Colored symbols show results from lattice-Boltzmann simulations.
L000264	   Inset in (a): master curve $\langle v_p \rangle / v_m = \sqrt{1-v_1^2/v_{*}^{2}} - 1$ with $v_* = 2v_m/\mathcal{G}_1$.
L000265	   In (b), (c) the magnitude $|\langle v_p \rangle|$ is plotted. Colors indicate values of $R_1/R_0$
L000266	   in (a) and $v_1/v_m$ in (b), (c),
L000267	
L000268	   while line types indicate values of
L000269	   $R_0/R_c$ in (a), (b) and $R_1/R_0$ in (c).
L000270	
L000271	         }
L000272	    	\label{fig:effect_R0Rc_R1R0}
L000273	\end{figure}
L000274	
L000275	
L000276	
L000277	
L000278	\emph{Full lubrication theory and lattice-Boltzmann simulations.}
L000279	In the full theory, the Fourier coefficients $\mathcal{G}_n$, and thereby $\langle v_p \rangle$, have a non-trivial dependence on
L000280	$R_1$ and $R_c$, which we now explore.  We also support our findings with lattice-Boltzmann simulations \cite{Krueger_book}.
L000281	In the following, we shall focus on the motion of ciliates with $v_0 =0$ since this case provides the clearest and physically most
L000282	interesting manifestation of the interplay between corrugation and slip velocity.
L000283	
L000284	First, in  Fig.\ \ref{fig:effect_R0Rc_R1R0}(a) we again observe the expected transition from the oscillatory to the phase-locked regime
L000285	with $\langle v_p \rangle = - v_m$ for increasing wave amplitude
L000286	$v_1$. As deduced from Eq.\ (\ref{eq.linearized}), all curves collapse on a master curve of the form  $\langle v_p \rangle / v_m = \sqrt{1-v_1^2/v_*^{2}}- 1$ with the critical wave amplitude
L000287	$v_* = 2v_m/\mathcal{G}_1$ separating the two dynamical regimes from each other [see inset of Fig.\ \ref{fig:effect_R0Rc_R1R0}(a)].
L000288	In Fig.\ \ref{fig:effect_R0Rc_R1R0}(b), the swimming velocity shows two local maxima for increasing corrugation
L000289	$R_1/R_0$. The maximum
L000290	at $R_1/R_0 \approx 1$ corresponds to the limiting case where fluid transport through the bottlenecks and along the entire ciliate is greatly
L000291	suppressed such that the velocity field instead resembles an array of vortices. Swimming in such a channel geometry is not due to fluid
L000292	transport from fore to aft, but due to an effective grip between the ciliate and the channel wall mediated by the enclosed pockets of fluid.
L000293	Surprisingly, between the two local maxima, a strong dip in $|\langle v_p \rangle|$ occurs at
L000294	$R_1^*/R_0$, the
L000295	value of which is mainly determined by $R_0/R_c$.
L000296	In fact, the instantaneous ciliate velocity becomes zero as well due to
L000297	a root of the function $\mathcal{G}_1(R_1/R_0)$
L000298	(see the Supplemental Material, Sect. II ).
L000299	No matter the phase shift between the wall corrugation and the metachronal wave, the cilia beatings in the fore and aft directions
L000300	compensate each other.
L000301	The dip in $v_p(t)$ arises when the system transitions between two flow morphologies. For $R_1/R_0$
L000302	below $R_1^*/R_0$,
L000303	the velocity field reveals a fluid layer that spans the
L000304	entire modulated channel and flows in the same direction as the ciliate [blue-shaded region in Fig.\ \ref{fig:purely_lubrication} (b)].
L000305	In the opposite case (e.g. $R_1/R_0=0.8$), the
L000306	size of this layer is greatly reduced,
L000307	and
L000308	the flow is dominated instead by large vortices within the pockets. At $R_1/R_0=R_1^*/R_0$,
L000309	the two flow types cancel each other and $\langle v_p \rangle$ drops to zero. More details can be found in the Supplemental Material together with illustrative Videos 4-6 . Finally, Fig.\ \ref{fig:effect_R0Rc_R1R0}(c) demonstrates that with increasing ciliate curvature
L000310	$R_0/R_c$, the swimming velocity decreases. Thus, ciliates with negligible curvature ($R_0/R_c \rightarrow 0$) swim fastest.
L000311	The presented curves are directly supported by lattice-Boltzmann simulations, as the symbols in Figs.~\ref{fig:effect_R0Rc_R1R0}(a)-(c) show.
L000312	
L000313	
L000314	Now, we relax the condition $\lambda_c = \lambda_w$ and show in Fig.\ \ref{fig:resonance}(a) that net swimming is also possible for the higher harmonics, $\lambda_w/\lambda_c =n$, as predicted by Eq.\ (\ref{eq_lubTheory}).
L000315	
L000316	The swimming velocity decreases with this ratio once the phase-locked regime with $v_p/v_m = -1$ is no longer reached.
L000317	
L000318	\begin{figure}[t]
L000319		\centering
L000320	   \includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth]{images/resonance.pdf}
L000321	   \caption{
L000322	   Resonance for finite ciliates. Time-averaged ciliate velocity $|\langle v_p \rangle|$ \emph{vs.} the ratio $\lambda_w/\lambda_c$ for various $v_1/v_m$. (a) Finite ciliate with length $L_c/\lambda_w \gg 1$. (b) Results from lattice-Boltzmann simulations for a finite, spherocylindrical ciliate with length $L_c / \lambda_c = 3.2$ and $d/L_c = 0.25$, where $d$
L000323	     is the surface-to-surface distance between the ciliate and its periodic image. For both panels, $R_0/R_c = 3/5$ and $R_1/R_0=1/3$. For panel (a) $R_0/\lambda_c=1/10$, and (b) $R_0/\lambda_w = 3/20$. }
L000324	    	\label{fig:resonance}
L000325	\end{figure}
L000326	
L000327	Finally, we relax the limit of a very long ciliate, which allows swimming motion only at the metachronal wavelengths $\lambda_c=\lambda_w/n$.
L000328	To demonstrate the corrugation-enabled motion also for finite ciliates, we perform lattice-Boltzmann simulations for ciliates shaped like spherocylinders with no-slip boundary conditions on the spherical half-caps, with a total length $L_c / \lambda_c = 3.2$ (Supplemental Video 3 ),
L000329	as well as for ellipsoidal-shaped ciliates (Supplemental Videos 7 and 8 ). We again observe oscillatory trajectories with net drift
L000330	for relevant lengths $R_0/\lambda_c = R_0/\lambda_w = 1$, where lubrication theory is no longer valid.
L000331	
L000332	
L000333	As expected, peaks in the time-averaged propulsion velocity exist around the integer ratios $\lambda_w/\lambda_c$ but now they have a finite width
L000334	[see Fig.\ \ref{fig:resonance}(b)]. Further interesting features are visible: motion is also possible for $\lambda_w/\lambda_c < 1$, a time-averaged speed larger than $v_m$ occurs, and the peak for $\lambda_w/\lambda_c = 3$ is suppressed. However, a more thorough investigation of the influence of $L_c / \lambda_c$ on the propulsion speed is beyond the scope of this Letter. As a final test, our results remain valid when expanding our model to include a metachronal wave, which propagates oblique to the $x$ axis. In such a case, the dynamical regimes we describe in this article appear when the channel shows indentations in the azimuthal direction which corkscrew along the $x$ axis, similar to the internal surface of a rifle's barrel (Supplemental Material in Sec. III/ Supplemental Vi\-deo 9 ).
L000335	
L000336	Using lubrication theory and lattice-Boltzmann simulations, we show that the explicit time dynamics of metachronal waves enables ciliates to swim in a corrugated channel, even if they do not move, on average, in a bulk fluid. This is due to a resonance between the local hydrodynamic resistance, arising from a spatially-varying radius of the channel, and the active flows generated by a sinusoidal metachronal wave. We
L000337	rationalize our findings
L000338	via the Adler equation that describes the transition from the oscillatory to
L000339	the steady swimming regime. The mean swimming velocity can even reverse compared to the bulk fluid.
L000340	
L000341	This reversal of direction of motion may be used to fabricate a filter or chromatograph, which blocks the passage of a microorganism with a metachronal wave matching the channel corrugation,
L000342	but allows other microorganisms to pass. Estimates for \textit{Paramecium} and a mutant show that the slow down and even reversal of the swimming velocity in a corrugated channel
L000343	may be observable (see End Matter).
L000344	Despite motivating our model via ciliated microorganisms, our work provides a deeper understanding of how swimmers with a spatio-temporal fluid forcing at their surface behave in a corrugated channel, regardless of the nature of this forcing. The mechanism described in this Letter is in fact general enough that we expect it to also apply to other living beings, which propel via a metachronal wave of forcing, such as the gait displayed by centipedes \cite{Rieu2024}, milipedes \cite{Garcia_2021}, or cockroaches \cite{Weihmann2017}.
L000345	For these terrestrial creatures, a modulated ground friction profile can play a role analogous to the spatially nonuniform channel radius. We further emphasize the applicability of our results to artificial swimmers/walkers which employ e.g. magnetically-actuated ciliary carpets \cite{Gu2020}, for which parameters like $v_1/v_0$ can be tuned. The mechanism described in this Letter thus opens a new avenue for environment-sensitive motility and environment-mediated control of robots moving in confinement.
