L000364	\section*{End Matter\\ Experimental predictions}
L000365	Despite the scarcity of experimental data for most ciliates
L000366	(particularly with respect to the metachronal parameters $\lambda_c$,
L000367	
L000368	$\omega$, and the velocity amplitude $v_1$),
L000369	
L000370	we are able to predict the corrugation needed
L000371	to reverse the
L000372	
L000373	direction of motion
L000374	
L000375	of
L000376	\textit{Paramecium}. The parameters $R_c$, $L_c$, $v_0$, $\lambda_c$, and $\omega$ \cite{Paramecium_Jana2012} are approximately known,
L000377	and the parameter $v_1/v_0$ may be estimated as $v_1/v_0 \approx 3$ upon comparison with the cilia covering other organisms such
L000378	as \textit{Tetrahymena} \cite{Akisato2025} and \textit{Plenaria} \cite{Rompolas2010}. We predict that with an estimated swimming velocity of
L000379	$\approx 784 \mathrm{\mu m/s}$\cite{Funfak2014} (in a microchannel without corrugation), a \textit{Paramecium} in a corrugated channel of
L000380	average radius $\approx 57  \mathrm{\mu m}$ ($R_0/R_c =1$ \cite{Paramecium_Jana2012}) will invert direction when $R_1/R_0 \gtrapprox 0.96 $.
L000381	The effect of the corrugation is, however, already seen when $R_1/R_0 = 0.9$, for which the
L000382	swimming
L000383	velocity is $\approx 65\%$
L000384	of the value in the bulk fluid.
L000385	
L000386	
L000387	
L000388	A more amenable case is that of a \textit{Paramecium} mutant showing a deformed ciliary beat, which leads to a smaller
L000389	swimming
L000390	speed of $\approx 196 \mathrm{\mu m}$ \cite{Funfak2014}. Assuming
L000391	that only
L000392	
L000393	 the average actuation
L000394	
L000395	 of
L000396	the fluid and not
L000397	
L000398	the beat amplitude
L000399	is affected,
L000400	yields $v_1/v_0 \approx 12$, and an inversion of
L000401	the swimming
L000402	direction when $R_1/R_0 \gtrapprox  0.875$. Furthermore, we predict a velocity $\approx 65\%$
L000403	of the bulk-fluid value
L000404	
L000405	for $R_1/R_0 \approx 0.35$ and $R_1/R_0 \approx 0.825$.
L000406	
L000407	All these numbers show that the slow down and even reversal of the swimming velocity in a corrugated channel
L000408	should be observable in an experiment.
